  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A potent upper level shortwave will pivot through \nthe Carolinas this evening, with an accompanying area of moderate \nlow-mid level frontogenesis. The area of enhanced lift associated \nwith the wave and frontal forcing will overlap with an area of \nmoderate low-mid level moistening and steep lapse rates this \nevening. While moisture isn't great, short-term guidance insists on \nshower development from around sunset this evening to around \nmidnight tonight. This appears to be a scenario where lift and \nslantwise instability should be able to overcome the lack of better \nmoisture, and make use of whatever moisture is available. Elevated \ninstability is forecast to be lower than last evening, therefore the \nrisk of lightning looks to be lower. Significant rainfall is not \nexpected with these showers.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong surface high will build in over the weekend \nand will be accompanied by a colder than normal airmass. This should \nsupport below to well below normal temperatures, both during the day \nand at night. Behind tonight's cold front, it now looks like there \nis just enough of a chance for winds to decouple well inland as lows \nfall into the low to mid 30s. This increases the odds for frost \ndevelopment, and a Frost Advisory has been issued for late tonight \ninto early Saturday morning for the western row of ENC counties. \nEast of there, low to mid 30s appears possible as well, but \nconfidence in winds decoupling enough for frost formation is lower, \ntherefore the Advisory does not include those counties. \n\nThe coldest thicknesses associated with this airmass will settle in \nSaturday night. With strong high pressure overhead and good \nradiational cooling conditions, the stage is set for lows in the \nupper 20s to low 30s for many inland areas away from the influence \nof water. A Freeze Watch remains in effect for those areas for \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. A Frost Advisory may be needed \nfor some counties bordering the watch area if winds decouple enough. \nThis weekend may end the frost/freeze program for some counties, but \nfor the rest, some frost/freeze potential may continue into early \nnext week as high pressure continues to support good radiational \ncooling conditions in tandem with cold low-level thicknesses. The \nairmass is forecast to moderate some, though, but probably not \ndrastically so given a mostly unchanged synoptic pattern.",
